1. Scope: The scope of a static variable depends on where it is declared. If declared inside a function, it has function scope and is only accessible within that function. If declared at the file level (outside any function), it has internal linkage and is only accessible within the same translation unit (i.e., the same source file).

2. Lifetime: Static variables are initialized only once, at the start of the program, and they retain their value between function calls. This is in contrast to automatic variables (local variables) that are created and destroyed each time a function is called.

3. Initialization: If not explicitly initialized, static variables are initialized to zero (for numeric types) or null (for pointers).

